---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { listings, avatar, fmtDate } from '../../lib/data';
const vw = listings.find(l => l.slug === 'dani-garcia--vaultwarden')!;
const bw = listings.find(l => l.slug === 'bitwarden--server')!;
const CHECKED = 'Oct 31, 2026';
const S = {
  bw: 'https://bitwarden.com/pricing/',
  bwBiz: 'https://bitwarden.com/pricing/business/',
  op: 'https://1password.com/pricing/password-manager',
  opBiz: 'https://1password.com/pricing/business',
  bwSelf: 'https://bitwarden.com/help/install-on-premise-linux/',
  bwLite: 'https://bitwarden.com/help/install-and-deploy-lite/',
  bwLicense: 'https://bitwarden.com/help/licensing-on-premise/',
  vw: 'https://github.com/dani-garcia/vaultwarden',
  do: 'https://www.digitalocean.com/pricing/droplets',
};
// US$ per month, yearly billing, list prices read Oct 31, 2026.
// Vaultwarden: DigitalOcean 1 GiB Droplet $6 + weekly backups at 20% ($1.20).
const rows = [
  { who: 'Bitwarden (cloud)', c: ['$0 Free, or $1.65 Premium', '$3.99 Families (6 users)', '$40 Teams ($4 a user)', '$100 Teams'] },
  { who: '1Password', c: ['$3.99 Individual ($2.99 first year for new customers)', '$5.99 Families (5 users; $4.49 first year)', '$24.95 Teams Starter Pack', '$224.75 Business ($8.99 a user; Starter Pack stops at 20)'] },
  { who: 'Vaultwarden on your server', c: ['$7.20', '$7.20', '$7.20', '$7.20'] },
];
const chart = [
  { label: '1Password Business', v: 224.75, t: '$224.75/mo' },
  { label: 'Bitwarden Teams', v: 100, t: '$100/mo' },
  { label: 'Vaultwarden + server', v: 7.2, t: '$7.20/mo + your time' },
];
const maxC = 240;
// Measured Oct 31, 2026 on my Linux test box: Vaultwarden 1.37.4, SQLite. RSS from /proc, 1 MB = 1,024 KB.
const mem = [
  { label: 'Just started', v: 29 },
  { label: '1,000 items added', v: 41 },
  { label: 'One full sync (peak)', v: 54 },
  { label: '2,000 syncs, 10 at once (peak)', v: 493 },
  { label: '60 s after that', v: 289 },
];
const maxM = 520;
const faq = [
  { q: 'Is Vaultwarden cheaper than Bitwarden?', a: `For a team, by a lot. On ${CHECKED} Bitwarden Teams was $4 a user a month with yearly billing, so $100 a month for 25 people. Vaultwarden is free software, and the $6 DigitalOcean server I’d run it on plus backups is $7.20 a month no matter how many users you add. For one person or a family it isn’t cheaper: Bitwarden Free costs nothing and Families is $3.99 a month for six people.` },
  { q: 'How much RAM does Vaultwarden need?', a: 'Very little for normal use. In my test Vaultwarden 1.37.4 used 29 MB right after starting, 41 MB with 1,000 vault items, and peaked at 54 MB during a full sync. When I hammered it with 2,000 full syncs from 10 clients at once it went up to 493 MB and was still holding 289 MB a minute later. A 1 GiB server is plenty for a family or a small team.' },
  { q: 'Can I use the Bitwarden apps with Vaultwarden?', a: 'Yes. Vaultwarden implements the Bitwarden client API, so the official Bitwarden browser extensions, desktop and mobile apps connect to it once you point them at your server’s address. It’s an unofficial project, though, and its README asks you not to report problems to Bitwarden’s support.' },
  { q: 'Is Bitwarden cheaper than 1Password?', a: `At every size I priced on ${CHECKED}. Bitwarden Premium was $1.65 a month against $3.99 for 1Password Individual (or $2.99 in your first year), Families $3.99 against $5.99, and for 25 users Bitwarden Teams came to $100 a month against $224.75 for 1Password Business. The one exception is a team of exactly 10 to 20 people: 1Password’s Teams Starter Pack is $24.95 for 10 users, cheaper than Bitwarden Teams at $40.` },
];
const schema = [
  { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'Article', headline: 'Bitwarden vs Vaultwarden vs 1Password: what a password manager costs for 1, 6, 10 and 25 people', dateModified: '2026-10-31', author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'SelfHost Office' }, mainEntityOfPage: new URL('/compare/bitwarden-vs-vaultwarden-vs-1password/', Astro.site).toString() },
  { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage', mainEntity: faq.map(x => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: x.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: x.a } })) },
];
---
<Base title="Bitwarden vs Vaultwarden vs 1Password (Oct 2026): real cost for 1 to 25 people" description="Prices read Oct 31, 2026 and a Vaultwarden test install: for 25 users 1Password Business is $224.75/mo, Bitwarden Teams $100, Vaultwarden on a $6 server about $7.20. Measured RAM: 29 MB idle." schema={schema} crumbs={[['Home', '/'], ['Bitwarden vs Vaultwarden vs 1Password', '/compare/bitwarden-vs-vaultwarden-vs-1password/']]}>
<div class="wrap page-head prose">
  <span class="eyebrow">Comparison · Prices read on {CHECKED}</span>
  <h1>Bitwarden vs Vaultwarden vs 1Password: what a password manager costs for 1, 6, 10 and 25 people</h1>
  <p class="muted">Three common choices: Bitwarden’s hosted plans, 1Password, and Vaultwarden, the community-built server that works with the Bitwarden apps and runs on your own machine. I read both official pricing pages on {CHECKED}, priced four group sizes, and ran Vaultwarden on a test machine to see how much memory it really uses. All prices are in US dollars as both companies list them.</p>
  <p class="note"><b>Disclosure:</b> no affiliate or referral links on this page. Bitwarden, 1Password and DigitalOcean don’t pay us. <a href="/disclosure/">How we make money</a>.</p>

  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Just you:</b> Bitwarden Free. Unlimited passwords and devices for $0. If you want the extras (built-in authenticator codes, file attachments, emergency access), Premium is $1.65 a month.</li>
    <li><b>A family:</b> Bitwarden Families at $3.99 a month for six people. Self-hosting Vaultwarden costs more than that once you pay for a server, so I wouldn’t do it to save money here.</li>
    <li><b>A small business with 10 to 20 people:</b> it’s close. 1Password’s Teams Starter Pack is $24.95 a month for 10 users, Bitwarden Teams is $40. Past 20 users 1Password jumps to its $8.99-a-user Business plan.</li>
    <li><b>A team that already runs a server:</b> Vaultwarden. About $7.20 a month at any size, and it used 29 to 54 MB of memory in normal use in my test.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<section style="padding-top:6px"><div class="wrap">
<h2>Monthly cost for a 25-person team</h2>
<figure class="chart" style="margin:0">
<svg viewBox="0 0 640 135" width="100%" role="img" aria-labelledby="pw-t">
  <title id="pw-t">Monthly cost for 25 users, prices read {CHECKED}: 1Password Business $224.75, Bitwarden Teams $100, Vaultwarden on a $6 DigitalOcean server with backups $7.20</title>
  {chart.map((b, i) => {
    const y = 8 + i * 40; const w = (b.v / maxC) * 340;
    return <g>
      <text x="0" y={y + 14} font-size="12" fill="currentColor">{b.label}</text>
      <rect x="160" y={y + 2} width={w} height="16" fill={b.label.startsWith('Vault') ? '#c2410c' : '#2f3a4a'} />
      <text x={166 + w} y={y + 15} font-size="12" fill="currentColor">{b.t}</text>
    </g>;
  })}
</svg>
<figcaption class="note">US$ per month with yearly billing, list prices read {CHECKED}. The Vaultwarden line is a DigitalOcean 1 GiB Droplet ($6) plus weekly backups (20%, $1.20). A domain name and your time are extra.</figcaption>
</figure>
</div></section>

<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap">
<h2>All four group sizes</h2>
<div class="compare-wrap"><table class="compare">
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Option</th><th scope="col">1 person</th><th scope="col">Family of 5–6</th><th scope="col">Team of 10</th><th scope="col">Team of 25</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>{rows.map(r => <tr><th scope="row">{r.who}</th>{r.c.map(c => <td>{c}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
</table></div>
<p class="note">Per month, yearly billing. Sources, all read {CHECKED}: <a href={S.bw}>Bitwarden pricing</a>, <a href={S.bwBiz}>Bitwarden business pricing</a>, <a href={S.op}>1Password personal pricing</a>, <a href={S.opBiz}>1Password business pricing</a>, <a href={S.do}>DigitalOcean Droplet pricing</a>.</p>
</div></section>

<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap prose">
<h2>What each price gets you</h2>
<h3>Bitwarden</h3>
<p>The Free plan has unlimited passwords on unlimited devices and lets you share with one other person. Premium ($19.80 a year, so $1.65 a month) adds the integrated authenticator, file attachments, emergency access and security reports. Families ($47.88 a year) is six Premium accounts with unlimited sharing. For businesses, Teams is $4 a user a month (event logs, directory sync, SCIM provisioning) and Enterprise is $6 (SSO, enterprise policies, self-hosting, and a free Families plan for every user). Both business prices are for yearly billing.</p>
<h3>1Password</h3>
<p>Individual is $3.99 a month and Families $5.99 for five people, both billed yearly. When I checked, new customers buying directly from 1Password.com got those for $2.99 and $4.49 in their first year; the discount doesn’t apply through the app stores. The Teams Starter Pack is $24.95 a month (yearly) for 10 users, with up to 10 more seats at $4.99 each, so it tops out at $74.85 for 20 people. Beyond that you need Business at $8.99 a user a month. 1Password has no free plan and no self-hosted option.</p>
<h3>Vaultwarden</h3>
<p><a href={S.vw}>Vaultwarden</a> is a separate, open-source (AGPL-3.0) server written in Rust that speaks the same API as Bitwarden’s, so the normal Bitwarden apps and browser extensions work with it. It includes the things Bitwarden charges for: organizations and sharing, attachments, Send, emergency access, and two-step login with authenticator apps, YubiKey, FIDO2 and Duo. The software is free; you pay for the server. Two things to know before you start: the web vault only works over HTTPS, so you need a domain and a certificate (the project recommends a reverse proxy), and it isn’t made by Bitwarden, so support comes from its own community, not Bitwarden’s.</p>
<h3>Bitwarden’s own self-hosted server</h3>
<p>You can also run Bitwarden’s official server, but it won’t save you license money. Bitwarden says <a href={S.bwLicense}>self-hosting is free but paid features need a license file</a> from a paid account, and its pricing page lists self-hosting under Enterprise for businesses. The standard install wants <a href={S.bwSelf}>2 GB of RAM at minimum and 4 GB recommended</a>, which is a $24 DigitalOcean server. There’s a lighter single-container version, <a href={S.bwLite}>Bitwarden lite</a>, that needs at least 200 MB, but Bitwarden says it’s for personal use and home labs, not businesses. I didn’t install either: both need Docker, which my test machine doesn’t have.</p>

<h2>What I measured</h2>
<p>On {CHECKED} I pulled the official <code>vaultwarden/server</code> image (version 1.37.4, built Oct 5, 2026) and ran the program from it on my Linux test machine with its default SQLite database. The image is about 90 MB to download and 288 MB unpacked, of which the program is 45 MB and the bundled web vault 144 MB. It was answering requests 188 milliseconds after I started it.</p>
<p>Then I created an account and added 1,000 login items through the same API the Bitwarden apps use. The apps encrypt everything before it reaches the server, so I sent random data in the same encrypted format; the server only stores it and can’t tell the difference. Adding the 1,000 items took 0.7 seconds and a full sync of the vault was a 1.5 MB download. Memory is the resident size the process reported.</p>
</div></section>

<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap">
<figure class="chart" style="margin:0">
<svg viewBox="0 0 640 215" width="100%" role="img" aria-labelledby="mem-t">
  <title id="mem-t">Vaultwarden 1.37.4 memory measured {CHECKED}: 29 MB after start, 41 MB with 1,000 items, 54 MB peak during one full sync, 493 MB peak during 2,000 syncs from 10 clients at once, 289 MB one minute later</title>
  {mem.map((b, i) => {
    const y = 8 + i * 40; const w = (b.v / maxM) * 340;
    return <g>
      <text x="0" y={y + 14} font-size="12" fill="currentColor">{b.label}</text>
      <rect x="210" y={y + 2} width={w} height="16" fill={b.v > 100 ? '#c2410c' : '#2f3a4a'} />
      <text x={216 + w} y={y + 15} font-size="12" fill="currentColor">{b.v} MB</text>
    </g>;
  })}
</svg>
<figcaption class="note">Resident memory of the Vaultwarden process, 1 MB = 1,024 KB, measured {CHECKED}. Red bars are the stress test, which is far beyond what a real team does.</figcaption>
</figure>
</div></section>

<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap prose">
<p>In normal use Vaultwarden stayed between 29 and 54 MB. To see what happens under heavy load I ran 8,000 requests from 10 clients at once, including 2,000 full syncs of the 1,000-item vault, in 7.6 seconds. Memory climbed to 493 MB and was still at 289 MB a minute later, which looks like memory the program keeps after a spike rather than a leak, though I didn’t run it long enough to prove that. Even a 25-person team opening their apps on Monday morning sends a few dozen syncs, not 2,000 in seconds. On disk the database was 8.6 MB after the test.</p>
<p>So the $6, 1 GiB server is enough. If you’re worried about a spike like mine, the $12, 2 GiB server leaves lots of room.</p>

<h2>Which I’d pick</h2>
<ul>
  <li><b>For myself or my family:</b> Bitwarden, Free or Families. It’s the cheapest option here and someone else handles uptime and backups for the one thing you can’t afford to lose.</li>
  <li><b>For a 10-person office with no IT person:</b> 1Password Teams Starter Pack at $24.95 or Bitwarden Teams at $40. Both are hosted, both have admin controls; I’d let the team try both apps during the free trials and pick the one they’ll actually use.</li>
  <li><b>For a team of 25 or more with someone who already runs servers:</b> Vaultwarden. Against Bitwarden Teams it saves about $93 a month at 25 users and the gap grows with every hire. Against 1Password Business it saves about $218.</li>
  <li><b>I wouldn’t self-host a password manager casually.</b> If the server dies and you have no working backup, the vault on it is gone. Test a restore before you move the team over.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Honest limits</h2>
<ul>
  <li>I tested Vaultwarden on my own test machine, not on a 1 GiB VPS, and without HTTPS or a reverse proxy in front.</li>
  <li>I added items through the API with placeholder encrypted data instead of using the real Bitwarden apps, so I didn’t check app compatibility myself.</li>
  <li>I didn’t install Bitwarden’s official server or Bitwarden lite, so their memory numbers here are Bitwarden’s published requirements, not my measurements.</li>
  <li>I didn’t review the security of any of the three. Vaultwarden is a community project, separate from Bitwarden.</li>
  <li>Prices change, and 1Password’s first-year discount is a promotion. The date at the top is when I read them; check again before you commit.</li>
</ul>

<h2>FAQ</h2>
{faq.map(x => <><h3>{x.q}</h3><p>{x.a}</p></>)}
</div></section>

